From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Sargison <sargo@sargo.cc>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 22:45:10 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 96/96] Plutonium: desktop settings and a display configuration
 tool in the switcher
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The switcher (Super+Tab) gets a SETTINGS group under its built-ins: Quick
settings, Wi-Fi and network, Bluetooth, Sound, Displays, Power and
Notifications. Each row has a live summary ("HomeNet", "Volume 40%",
"Battery 80%, charging"), and each page drives the helper's desktop
services over its Unix socket (GET /v1/system/services, POST
/v1/system/services/<name>/<action>), the same ones the shells' bars use:
Wi-Fi on and off, networks with a password field for a new one, connect,
disconnect, forget; Bluetooth power, finding, pairing, connecting,
forgetting; volume, mute, the default output and input; brightness; now
playing; do not disturb and the notifications; lock, suspend, restart,
power off and log out (the last three after a second click); the idle
times. Sliders send one request at a time, the latest value next, and
aren't moved by the state while the user is on them. The services are
polled every second while the switcher is open.

Displays is a display configuration tool over Ash's CrosDisplayConfigImpl
(PlutoniumDisplayConfig): the displays drawn as they're arranged, to drag
into place (each goes against the nearest edge of one already placed, from
the primary), or to put left of, right of, above or below the primary from
the keyboard; and for each display its resolution, refresh rate, size
(Ash's zoom levels), rotation, primary, mirroring, and the built-in panel
off while a monitor is on (DisplayConfigurator's power state, which the
lock's "displays on" keeps). A new mode, size, rotation, mirroring or the
panel off goes back after 15 s unless kept: shell mode never shows Ash's
own confirmation, so modes are set as policy and the switcher asks.

The Plutonium DevTools domain gets getDisplayConfig, configureDisplays,
openSwitcher (a page: settings, displays, network, …) and the
displayConfigChanged event, for the helper's display service: the shells'
bars open the switcher's Displays page through it, and agents can read
and change the configuration.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
